CONTEXT FROM TEXTBOOK:
This figure illustrates the geometric relationship between heterogeneous (standard 2D) and homogeneous (projective) coordinate representations of the same 2D point. On the left, a point (x, y) exists in the standard Euclidean plane with axes x and y; on the right, the homogeneous coordinate space introduces a third axis w, and the canonical embedding of (x, y) is the point (x, y, 1) on the w=1 plane. A dashed ray extends from the origin through (x, y, 1) out to the general scalar multiple (lambda*x, lambda*y, lambda), showing that the entire ray through the origin corresponds to a single heterogeneous point, making scale irrelevant. This figure introduces the equivalence-class interpretation of homogeneous coordinates, which is the geometric foundation that allows projective transformations such as homographies to be expressed as linear matrix multiplications.

PROJECTION TYPE: axonometric
Use ordinary orthographic projection: setCameraView({ projection: 'orthographic', axisScreenAngles, heightFraction }). If the plan gives axis_screen_angles: null (a flat 2D plot/diagram), pass axisScreenAngles: null for a front-on view. Otherwise the plan does not measure per-figure angles — use a standard isometric pose: axisScreenAngles: { x: 30, z: 30 }. The source image is a PROJECTION of a 3D object: rebuild the true object — a rectangle stays a rectangle in world space — and let the camera do the projecting. Never bake the drawn parallelogram into the geometry, and never shear geometry. Omit zoom and call setCameraView after all geometry is added — the scaffold auto-fits from the projected bounding box.

ELEMENTS TO RECREATE IN 3D:
  - y-axis: vertical black arrow pointing up from origin, labeled 'y' at top
  - w-axis: horizontal black arrow pointing right from origin, labeled 'w' at right end
  - x-axis: black arrow receding up-and-right on a diagonal from origin (out of the w=0 plane toward the viewer), labeled 'x'; drawn as a full line extending down-left past the origin
  - tick mark on the w-axis at w = 1, labeled '1' below the axis
  - w = 0 plane (heterogeneous plane): light mint-gray filled parallelogram spanned by the x and y axes, on the left
  - w = 1 plane: gray outlined (unfilled) parallelogram, parallel to the w=0 plane, crossing the w-axis at the '1' tick
  - red dot for the 2D point (x, y) lying in the w = 0 plane, with label '(x,y)' above-left of it
  - thin gray horizontal connector segment from (x, y) to (x, y, 1), showing the lift along w
  - red dot for the canonical homogeneous point (x, y, 1) lying on the w = 1 plane, label '(x,y,1)' below-right of it
  - black dashed ray starting at the origin, passing through (x, y, 1) and continuing up-right beyond the w=1 plane
  - red dot for the scaled representative at the far end of the dashed ray, label '(λx, λy, λ)' above it
  - text label 'Heteregeneous' at far left, level with the x-axis label
  - text label 'Homogeneous' at far right, level with the x-axis label

GEOMETRY NOTES (world-space scale, color palette, and any formula/value needed for correctness — build to these so sizes, zoom, and colors match the source; do not guess dimensions the notes already specify):
World axes: +X is screen-right and carries the w coordinate (w-axis drawn from w = -0.4 to w = 3.6, arrowhead at the right end, tick at w = 1 as a short vertical bar with label '1' just below the axis). +Y is screen-up and carries the y coordinate (y-axis from y = -2.4 to y = +2.4, arrowhead only at the top). +Z is the receding diagonal drawn up-and-right toward the viewer and carries the x coordinate (x-axis drawn as a full line from x = -2.6 to x = +2.0 with an arrowhead at the +x end; on screen it rises at roughly 40° above horizontal). The two planes are parallel to the y–x plane (constant w): w = 0 plane is a filled light mint-gray parallelogram (#E3EDE9, ~55% opacity, no stroke) spanning y ∈ [-2.2, 2.2], x ∈ [-2.2, 1.6]; the w = 1 plane is the same parallelogram translated to w = 1, drawn as a gray outline only (#66807C, ~4px, unfilled). Red points are #EE1B18 spheres of radius ~0.1 with a thin black rim. Default point: x = 0.55, y = 1.15 (so (x,y) sits just above and left of where the x-axis arrow crosses the w=0 plane, matching the source). Canonical point at (w=1, y=1.15, x=0.55) on the w=1 plane. Thin gray connector (#555, 1.5px) from (x,y) in the w=0 plane straight along +w to the canonical point — on screen it reads nearly horizontal. Dashed ray: black, dash ~0.12/0.10, from the origin (0,0,0) through (x·λ, y·λ, λ) with λ running to 2.8; the far representative dot sits at λ_slider (default 1.0 coincident with the canonical point; demo pushes it to 2.4). Labels in a serif italic math face, black: 'y' above the y arrow, 'x' above-left of the x arrow tip, 'w' below-right of the w arrow tip, '(x,y)' up-left of the left red dot, '(x,y,1)' down-right of the canonical dot, '(λx,λy,λ)' above the far dot. Plain serif labels 'Heteregeneous' at far left and 'Homogeneous' at far right, both roughly at the height of the x-axis label. Recovery formula to display: (x/w, y/w); in equivalence-class mode draw extra ghost dots at λ = 0.5, 1, 2 along the ray in a lighter red (#F08080) with tiny labels (0.5x,0.5y,0.5), (x,y,1), (2x,2y,2).

DISCRETE CONTROLS (implement every one of these in #ui, each must work independently):
[
  {
    "id": "dragPoint",
    "type": "drag",
    "teaches": "Moving the 2D point (x,y) inside the w=0 plane swings the whole homogeneous ray about the origin, while its canonical representative stays pinned on the w=1 plane — every 2D point names one ray, not one 3D point."
  },
  {
    "id": "lambdaSlider",
    "type": "slider",
    "teaches": "Sliding the representative along the ray changes (λx, λy, λ) but dividing through by w always returns the same heterogeneous point (x/w, y/w) — scale carries no information.",
    "label": "λ (value of w for the selected representative)",
    "range": [
      0.25,
      3,
      0.05
    ],
    "default": 1
  },
  {
    "id": "classView",
    "type": "toggle",
    "teaches": "The ray is a single equivalence class: several scaled points such as (x,y,1), (2x,2y,2), (0.5x,0.5y,0.5) all project back to the one left-plane point.",
    "label": "View",
    "options": [
      "Canonical only",
      "Equivalence class"
    ],
    "default": "Canonical only"
  },
  {
    "id": "orbitScene",
    "type": "orbit",
    "teaches": "Rotating the scene shows that the dashed line really does pass through the 3D origin and pierces the w=1 plane exactly once, which is why the projective representation is well defined."
  }
]

DEMO STEPS (tween through these using goToStep(); each step drives the controls above):
[
  {
    "title": "Canonical lift",
    "narration": "The point (x, y) in the left heterogeneous plane is lifted straight out along w to the canonical homogeneous point (x, y, 1) on the w = 1 plane.",
    "state": {
      "lambdaSlider": 1,
      "classView": "Canonical only"
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Slide along the ray",
    "narration": "Pushing the representative out to λ = 2.4 gives (λx, λy, λ). It is a different 3D point, yet dividing by w recovers exactly the same (x, y) on the left.",
    "state": {
      "lambdaSlider": 2.4,
      "classView": "Canonical only"
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "One ray, one point",
    "narration": "Highlighting several scaled copies at once shows the whole ray through the origin as a single equivalence class mapping to one heterogeneous point.",
    "state": {
      "lambdaSlider": 2,
      "classView": "Equivalence class"
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Shrink toward the origin",
    "narration": "As λ approaches 0 the representative collapses toward the origin; the direction survives but the heterogeneous coordinates blow up — this is how points at infinity appear.",
    "state": {
      "lambdaSlider": 0.3,
      "classView": "Equivalence class"
    }
  }
]

CAMERA VIEW (framing only). Call setCameraView({ projection, ...these }) AFTER all geometry is added. Keep the projection MECHANISM from PROJECTION TYPE above (orthographic / sheared-oblique / perspective). For perspective these params set the pose (azimuth/elevation/roll); for the two parallel projections pose is a standard default (see PROJECTION TYPE above), and these params set only roll and framing (heightFraction):
{
  "heightFraction": 0.65
}

NOTES: Use an orthographic camera posed isometrically (w to screen-right, y up, x receding up-right) so parallel planes stay parallel and the two parallelograms are congruent. Planes: PlaneGeometry with MeshBasicMaterial (DoubleSide, transparent) for w=0; LineLoop/EdgesGeometry for the w=1 outline so it stays unfilled and its far edge shows through. Axes and arrowheads: thick Line2/LineMaterial (linewidth in screen px) plus small ConeGeometry tips, rendered with depthWrite off so they read crisply over the mint plane. Dashed ray: LineDashedMaterial with computeLineDistances, or Line2 with dashed option for consistent screen-space dashes. Labels as CSS2DRenderer HTML with KaTeX/italic serif so they stay upright and legible while orbiting. Dragging: raycast against an invisible plane coincident with w=0 and clamp (x,y) to the parallelogram bounds; recompute the ray, canonical point, connector and all scaled ghost dots each frame. Render red dots as Sphere meshes (not sprites) so orbit reveals their depth ordering; draw a subtle dashed drop line from each dot to the w axis if helpful. Keep a readout panel showing w = λ and the recovered (x/w, y/w).

Follow the interaction plan above.

